<!--
    Kontur Code design system — motion tokens.

    Motion is part of the type and colour system, not per-view decoration: the same
    durations and the same decelerating curve everywhere, so the product moves with one
    voice. Two budgets:

      - Interaction (hover, press, selection): 90-140ms. Fast enough to feel like a
        property of the pointer, not an effect the app plays.
      - Presence (overlays, panels, entrances): 180-240ms, decelerating. The interface
        ends still; nothing bounces, nothing lingers.

    All curves are one cubic-bezier family: strong ease-out, meaning the animation
    spends its energy early and settles. Linear reads mechanical; ease-in reads lag.

    Every storyboard that consumes these tokens also checks the system's animation
    preference (Motion.cs does for attached behaviours; storyboards here stay under
    250ms, which is the accessibility-safe band on its own).
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- Durations. Declared as Duration resources rather than raw TimeSpans: the
         animation properties consume them directly, no converter in the middle. -->
    <Duration x:Key="Anim.Instant">0:0:0.09</Duration>
    <Duration x:Key="Anim.Fast">0:0:0.13</Duration>
    <Duration x:Key="Anim.Normal">0:0:0.18</Duration>
    <Duration x:Key="Anim.Slow">0:0:0.24</Duration>

    <!-- The one curve: strong decelerate. EaseOut cubic - the animation spends its
         energy early and settles, which reads as arrival rather than travel. -->
    <CubicEase x:Key="Anim.EaseOut" EasingMode="EaseOut" />

</ResourceDictionary>
